update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"Some of the most damaging maturity assessments are the ones that are technically excellent. Picture a consultancy that grades a mid-sized company against a full staged model, produces a beautiful report card, and stamps it \"Level 1 — Initial.\" The engineers who have been quietly holding quality together for years read that as an insult, disengage, and the improvement programme dies on contact. The numbers may well be right. The framing is fatal. Maturity that lands as a verdict on people, rather than a diagnosis of a system, does more harm than no assessment at all — because you only get one first read from a sceptical engineering org, and spending it telling them they are a \"1\" is a wound you cannot easily undo.","visualAid":{"type":"comparison","title":"Staged vs continuous maturity models","headers":["Aspect","Staged (e.g. TMMi)","Continuous (e.g. TPI Next)"],"rows":[["Answers the question","What single level are we?","How strong is each capability?"],["Improvement shape","Advance the whole org up a ladder","Attack specific weak areas independently"],["Best when","Audit, certification, regulated context","Diagnosis and prioritisation"],["Risk it invites","\"Race to Level 5\" vanity","Fragmentation without a narrative"],["Fit for a new QA leader","Heavy; slow to first insight","Fast, actionable, mirrors messy reality"]]},"davidTip":"For a new QA leader, the first habit worth breaking is treating the maturity number as a target. Your exec sponsor does not care whether you are a \"2\" or a \"3\" — they care whether the next release will embarrass them and whether you are spending their money on the right risks. So do the assessment rigorously, but translate every finding into risk and money before it leaves your mouth. And be brave enough to write \"recommend no investment here — risk is low\" next to a weak area. That single line, more than any improvement plan, is what tells a sharp VP that you actually understand prioritisation.","badGood":{"label":"opening a maturity assessment with leadership","bad":"\"I've assessed our QA and we're only at Level 1 out of 5, so we need a programme to get us to Level 3 this year.\" — reads as a number chase and a headcount grab; invites the \"empire-building QA\" reaction.","good":"\"I've mapped where our quality risk is concentrated across a dozen capability areas. Two of them — test environments and defect signal — are actively costing us release speed and customer trust. A couple of others are weak but low-risk, and I'm recommending we leave those alone. Here's the evidence.\" — a risk-and-investment conversation the exec can act on."},"miniChallenge":"Northstar's CTO says: \"I've read about TMMi. Let's get to Level 3 by year end — put a plan together.\" You believe a blanket ladder-climb is the wrong move here. In three sentences, respond in a way that respects the CTO's intent, reframes the goal around risk and context, and keeps your credibility. What do you say?","modelAnswer":"## Example\n\"I love that you want a concrete target — let me make it one we can defend to the board. Rather than moving the whole function up a ladder, I'll diagnose each capability and show you which two or three are genuinely costing us speed and incidents; those are where a Level-3-style investment pays back. For the areas where our risk is low, I'll recommend we deliberately stay lean, so we're not buying process we don't need — that's the version of 'Level 3' that actually protects the release date you care about.\" This keeps the CTO's appetite for a target, converts it from a vanity number into a risk-justified investment, and signals that you prioritise rather than gold-plate.","portfolioBuilder":"This module's portfolio output is a QA Current-State Assessment of Northstar. Begin it now by writing its opening framing page: a two-paragraph statement of purpose that defines maturity as predictable capability, commits to a continuous (per-capability) diagnostic, and states explicitly that the goal is to right-size investment to risk — not to reach a top level. Hiring managers read this page first; it proves you lead with judgement, not jargon.","resourcePreview":{"name":"QA Maturity Frame — One-Pager","purpose":"A concise reference contrasting staged vs continuous models and listing the capability dimensions this module assesses, with the \"right maturity = risk + context\" principle printed at the top.","whenToUse":"Keep it beside you through Module 2 and reuse it to brief a leadership team before any real assessment.","formats":["PDF"]}}$ench$::jsonb
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where l.module_id = m.id and c.slug = 'qa-leadership-academy'
