update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"It is common to see an organisation spend a serious sum on a new test-automation platform to \"fix quality\", change nothing about who owned or wrote the tests, and arrive back at exactly the same flakiness and distrust a year later — now with a bigger licence bill. The tool was never the constraint. Ownership was. The pattern is so reliable that when someone says \"we just need a better tool,\" the seasoned response is to treat it as evidence of a culture diagnosis waiting to be uncovered, not as a technology requirement to be fulfilled.","visualAid":{"type":"matrix","title":"Four lenses × what to gather","colLabels":["What you assess","Evidence to gather","The trap"],"rowLabels":["People","Process","Technology","Culture"],"cells":[["Skills, coverage, key-person risk, progression","Interviews, skills matrix, org vs demand, attrition","Counting heads instead of matching skills to risk"],["How work really flows, gates, defect signal, regression","Walk a real change, read tickets, time the cycle","Reading the wiki instead of watching reality"],["Automation trust, CI/CD, environments, test data","Pipeline config, flaky rates, blocked-time log","Buying tools to fix people/culture gaps"],["Ownership, safety, attitude to QA, response to failure","Last incident, who is praised, who is in refinement","Skipping it as \"unmeasurable\""]]},"davidTip":"The four lenses are also a self-defence tool against your own CV. Every QA leader has a home lens — if yours leans towards process and culture, you have to consciously force yourself to interrogate the tooling and the skills breadth before you conclude; if it leans towards automation, the reverse. When you write up an assessment, look at your recommendations and ask: \"have I just recommended more of the thing I personally happen to be good at?\" If all your fixes are automation, or all of them are process, that is a warning that you assessed through one lens and called it the whole system.","badGood":{"label":"diagnosing low trust in the automation suite","bad":"\"The Selenium suite is flaky and slow — we need to migrate to a modern framework.\" — a single-lens (technology) diagnosis that ignores ownership, skills concentration, and process.","good":"\"The suite is flaky (technology), but the governing cause is that developers disown it (culture) and one person maintains it (people). I recommend shifting critical-path ownership into the squads and rebalancing toward API-level checks before we spend anything on tooling.\" — names the dominant lens and sequences the fix."},"miniChallenge":"You interview Sofia, the senior QA who is wary of change after a failed \"automate everything\" push last year. She says: \"We tried new tools before. It didn't work.\" Which lens is her comment really about, and what does it tell you about whether a technology-led improvement will stick this time?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nSofia's comment looks like a technology data point but is really a **culture** signal: a previous change was imposed as a tooling initiative without shifting ownership or bringing the team with it, so it failed and left scar tissue. It tells me that any technology-led improvement I attempt now will hit the same wall unless I first address the cultural conditions — shared ownership, involving the team in the design, and a smaller, provable win to rebuild trust. The right move is to treat Sofia as a partner in diagnosis, not an obstacle: her scepticism is accurate evidence that last time we changed the tool but not the system around it.","managersReview":{"intro":"When a manager reviews your four-lens assessment, here is what earns their confidence and where these assessments usually go wrong:","strengths":["You gather concrete evidence under each lens rather than asserting opinions","You name the dominant lens for each symptom instead of listing complaints","You resist the reflex to recommend a tool as the answer to a people or culture problem"],"gaps":["Assessing through your own background and calling it the whole system","Skipping culture because it feels unmeasurable","Confusing ratios (testers per developer) with a real skills-vs-risk analysis"],"improvements":["For every technology recommendation, write the culture and people conditions it depends on","Convert at least one cultural belief into an observable behaviour you actually measured","Sequence your recommendations so ownership shifts precede tooling spend"]},"portfolioBuilder":"Add the four-lens assessment to your Northstar QA Current-State Assessment. For each lens, write a short findings section grounded in specific Northstar evidence (the 6-QA-to-35-dev ratio, the Figma-link requirements, the disowned Selenium suite, the \"QA doesn't add value\" belief), and end with one sentence naming the dominant lens overall. This section is the analytical heart of the portfolio deliverable.","resourcePreview":{"name":"Four-Lens Capability Assessment Template","purpose":"A structured template with prompts and evidence checklists for people, process, technology and culture, plus a column for naming the governing lens behind each symptom.","whenToUse":"Use it to run a real capability assessment and to build the analysis section of your Current-State Assessment.","formats":["PDF","Markdown"]}}$ench$::jsonb
