update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"The first time a delivery flow is mapped *with* a team rather than *for* them, the map stops being the assessor's and becomes theirs. Stick it on a wall, and within twenty minutes the developers will often be arguing — cheerfully — about which queue is worst. That is the real power of mapping the actual SDLC: it moves the conversation from \"is QA any good?\" to \"look at how long our own changes sit waiting.\" Once the team is arguing about the system instead of defending themselves, you have already won the hardest part of the assessment.","visualAid":{"type":"flow","title":"A Northstar change from idea to production (real flow)","steps":[{"label":"Thin ticket","detail":"a Figma link and a sentence — defects injected here go undetected for weeks"},{"label":"Queue","detail":"waiting for a developer to pick it up"},{"label":"Develop","detail":"code + unit tests on PR — the only early quality feedback"},{"label":"Hand-off to QA","detail":"QA first sees the change at code-complete — quality inspected in, not built in"},{"label":"Environment + data wait","detail":"waiting for a stable staging slot and rebuilding overwritten test data"},{"label":"Test + rework loop","detail":"defects rooted in the vague requirement bounce back to development"},{"label":"5-day manual regression","detail":"a single huge late stage that dominates lead time"},{"label":"Human-approved deploy","detail":"semi-automated release gated on manual judgement"}]},"davidTip":"Do not let anyone talk you out of putting time on the map. A value-stream map without lead time and process time is just a flow chart, and flow charts change nothing. The number that moves executives is flow efficiency — when they see that a change spends, say, 15% of its life being worked on and 85% waiting, the conversation stops being about whether QA is slow and starts being about how the whole system leaks time. That single ratio unlocks more QA investment than any test-coverage figure ever will.","badGood":{"label":"presenting the SDLC to leadership","bad":"\"Here's our process: refine, build, test, regress, release.\" — a tidy diagram with no time, no queues, and no indication of where quality is lost.","good":"\"Here's a real change we shipped: 4 days of work spread across 22 elapsed days. Defects were injected in the requirement and not caught until after code-complete, then a 5-day manual regression sat at the very end. We lose time and quality in the queues, not in the work.\" — a map that diagnoses."},"miniChallenge":"Tom, Head of Product, sees your map and says: \"So QA is the bottleneck — that 5-day regression is killing my release dates. Can you just make it faster?\" The map actually shows the regression is a symptom of defects injected far upstream. In two or three sentences, how do you respond without either accepting blame or dismissing Tom's frustration?","modelAnswer":"## Example\n\"You're right that the 5-day regression is the most visible cost, and I want to shrink it — but the map shows why it's so big: we're catching at the end the defects we injected in vague requirements at the very start, so regression is doing the work our early stages should have. If we get QA into refinement and build automated checks in as we go, the regression gets smaller because there's less to catch late — that's how we protect your dates sustainably rather than just rushing the last gate.\" This validates Tom's concern, reframes regression as a downstream symptom, and offers a fix that serves his actual goal (predictable release dates) rather than defending QA.","portfolioBuilder":"Add the SDLC value-stream map to your Northstar QA Current-State Assessment. Include the real-change walkthrough, process time versus lead time with a flow-efficiency estimate, and the QA-touchpoint overlay showing where quality is inspected in rather than built in. This is the visual centrepiece of the deliverable — the artefact a hiring manager will remember.","resourcePreview":{"name":"Value-Stream Mapping Kit for QA","purpose":"A worksheet and legend for mapping a real change, capturing queues and rework, computing lead versus process time, and overlaying QA touchpoints and quality gates.","whenToUse":"Use it live in a mapping session with a delivery team and to produce the map section of your Current-State Assessment.","formats":["PDF","Markdown"]}}$ench$::jsonb
