update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"Almost every team has a \"known\" bottleneck — and roughly half the time the known bottleneck is wrong. It is almost always the most *visible* pain, not the actual constraint, because visible pain is what people complain about in stand-ups. The discipline that rarely fails is refusing to fix anything until you can answer, with evidence, \"if this were instant tomorrow, would we ship faster?\" More improvement budgets are wasted on relieving non-constraints than on almost any other single mistake QA leaders make.","visualAid":{"type":"tree","title":"Is this stage the real constraint?","branches":[{"condition":"Does work consistently queue in front of it?","outcome":"If no → it is not the constraint; keep looking"},{"condition":"Does the whole flow move no faster than this stage?","outcome":"If no → it is a secondary problem, not the governing one"},{"condition":"If it were instantaneous tomorrow, would total lead time fall?","outcome":"If no → work would just pile up elsewhere; this is a symptom"},{"condition":"All three yes?","outcome":"This is your constraint — exploit it free first, then elevate with investment"},{"condition":"Relieved it and lead time did not move?","outcome":"You were wrong — the constraint moved; reassess and repeat"}]},"davidTip":"The bravest and most valuable thing a new QA leader can do is refuse to accept the blame for the regression on day one. It is tempting to promise \"I'll make the regression faster\" because it makes you look responsive. But if you fix the symptom and lead time does not move, you own a failure that was never yours to begin with. Instead, buy the cheap win — risk-based regression — to show good faith and immediate value, and in the same breath present the evidence for the real upstream constraint. Responsiveness plus a correct diagnosis is how you build the credibility to get the expensive fix funded later.","badGood":{"label":"responding to \"QA is the bottleneck\"","bad":"\"You're right, the regression is too slow — I'll make my team run it faster.\" — accepts a displaced diagnosis, commits QA to fixing a symptom, and will fail to move lead time.","good":"\"The regression is the most visible cost, and I'll cut it immediately with risk-based selection. But the evidence shows it's long because defects are injected upstream and caught late — so the durable fix is a stable environment and earlier feedback. Here's the data.\" — buys a fast win and redirects to the true constraint."},"miniChallenge":"You have narrowed Northstar's candidates to two: the unstable shared staging environment and the late QA involvement. You can only make the case for elevating (investing in) one this quarter. Which do you argue for first, and what is your reasoning about sequence and leverage?","modelAnswer":"## Example\nI argue for the environment first, on both leverage and sequence grounds. The unstable staging is a hard constraint that blocks every squad's testing regardless of when QA engages — you literally cannot get reliable feedback earlier if there is nowhere stable to run it, so fixing environments is a prerequisite that unblocks the later fix. Late QA involvement is arguably the deeper cause, but relieving it depends on having a dependable place to test and trustworthy fast checks to run; sequencing the environment first makes the earlier-feedback investment actually land instead of stalling on the same contention. I would still name late involvement as the next constraint to elevate, so leadership sees the sequence, not a one-off fix.","portfolioBuilder":"Add a bottleneck analysis to your Northstar QA Current-State Assessment. List the candidate constraints, apply the disproving question to each, name the single governing constraint (with evidence from your map), and state the \"exploit now / elevate next\" sequence. Include the prediction of where the constraint will move once relieved — that forward-looking line is what marks the analysis as senior.","resourcePreview":{"name":"Constraint-Finder Worksheet","purpose":"A worksheet that runs each queuing stage through the disproving question and the five focusing steps, producing a ranked, evidenced shortlist of candidate constraints.","whenToUse":"Use it immediately after value-stream mapping to move from \"list of problems\" to \"the one constraint to attack\".","formats":["PDF","Markdown"]}}$ench$::jsonb
