update public.lessons l set enhancements = $ench${"industryStory":"The gap analyses that get funded are rarely the longest ones. They are the ones with a short, confident \"we are deliberately not doing these, and here's why\" section. Executives are wary of QA leaders who present an infinite list of weaknesses, because it reads as either bottomless cost or blame-shifting. The moment you show that you have already said no to several tempting improvements on risk grounds, you stop looking like someone asking for a blank cheque and start looking like someone they can trust with a budget. Restraint, written down, is a credibility signal.","visualAid":{"type":"matrix","title":"Northstar capability gaps — target set by risk","colLabels":["Current","Risk-derived target","Closure approach"],"rowLabels":["API / contract automation","Performance","Exploratory testing","Accessibility","Security"],"cells":[["Very low","High (public API sold on SLAs)","Build — retrain manual testers toward the API layer"],["None","High (SLA + payments risk)","Borrow a baseline now, then build into the team"],["Strong","High (already met)","Protect and spread — reduce key-person risk on Sofia"],["None","Moderate","Not now — parked, revisit when B2B needs it"],["None","Moderate","Not now — mitigate via provider + borrow if needed"]]},"davidTip":"When you write build/buy/borrow, resist the reflex to hire. Hiring feels like the decisive, leaderly move, but a single senior hire into a gap often just relocates the key-person risk you were trying to remove — now the whole capability walks out of the door if that one person leaves. A sound default for a genuinely absent capability is borrow-then-build: bring in short-term expertise to establish the baseline and the standards, and use that engagement to level up two of your own people so the capability actually lives in the team afterwards. It is usually cheaper, and it leaves you more resilient than a hire would.","badGood":{"label":"requesting investment to close a capability gap","bad":"\"We have no performance, security, or accessibility capability and we're understaffed — we need three specialist hires.\" — a list of everything missing, defaulting to hire, with no risk prioritisation.","good":"\"Two gaps actually threaten our SLA promise: API-contract testing and performance. I'll build the first by retraining manual testers and borrow-then-build the second. Accessibility and dedicated security are real but lower-risk — I'm parking them this quarter and will flag when that changes.\" — prioritised, sequenced, and explicit about restraint."},"miniChallenge":"Marcus (Payments lead), still sceptical of QA, says: \"If your team was any good you wouldn't need to hire or bring in contractors — you'd just do it.\" Your performance-testing gap is real and closing it needs outside expertise Northstar genuinely lacks. How do you respond in a way that neither caves nor escalates the conflict?","modelAnswer":"## Example\n\"You're right that I don't want to just throw hires at this — that's exactly why I'm proposing we borrow a performance specialist for one quarter rather than hire one permanently. We have strong testers, but nobody has built a performance harness against an SLA before, and pretending otherwise is how we'd end up promising the B2B partners something we can't actually validate. The contractor sets the baseline and, more importantly, levels up two of my people so the capability stays in the team — that's cheaper and more resilient than a hire, and it directly protects the payments path you own.\" This concedes Marcus's real point (don't over-hire), reframes borrowing as disciplined rather than weak, and ties the capability straight to a risk he cares about, defusing the challenge instead of fighting it.","managersReview":{"intro":"Reviewing a capability gap analysis, here is what separates a fundable plan from a wish list:","strengths":["Targets are derived from business risk and set per capability, not flat","The current-state matrix is evidenced and honest about key-person risk","Every gap carries an impact statement and a build/buy/borrow option with its trade-off"],"gaps":["A flat \"everyone to a high level\" target that manufactures work","Optimistic self-scoring that shrinks the gaps and dooms the plan","Defaulting to \"hire\" for every gap and recreating key-person risk"],"improvements":["Set and write down the target state before measuring current state","Add an explicit \"deliberately not closing\" list with one-line risk justifications","Sequence gap closure to relieve the true constraint from Lesson 4 first"]},"portfolioBuilder":"Add the capability gap analysis to your Northstar QA Current-State Assessment: the risk-derived target profile, the evidenced skills matrix, the top two or three prioritised gaps with build/buy/borrow recommendations and trade-offs, and the explicit \"not now\" list. This section proves you can turn a diagnosis into a defensible, budget-aware plan — the capability a hiring manager is really testing for.","resourcePreview":{"name":"Skills Matrix & Gap Analysis Toolkit","purpose":"A skills-matrix template plus a gap-analysis sheet that forces a risk-derived target, distance/impact per gap, a build/buy/borrow decision, and a deliberately-unclosed list.","whenToUse":"Use it to run a real capability assessment and to build the gap-analysis section of your Current-State Assessment.","formats":["XLSX","PDF","Markdown"]}}$ench$::jsonb
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
